<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Herod Antipas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 20 BCE–after 39 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Herod Antipas, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-the-great">Herod the Great</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peraea">Peraea</a> as tetrarch from 4 BCE to 39 CE. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberias">Tiberias</a> as his capital, had <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> executed and appears in the Gospel accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a>'s trial. In 39 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caligula">Caligula</a> deposed and exiled him, giving his lands to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-agrippa-i">Herod Agrippa I</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/herod-antipas.jpg" alt="Herod Antipas" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Georges Reverdy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Herod_Antipas_medal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
